75, -0.00846099853515625, 0.00945281982421875, 0.0008816719055175781, 0.0174102783203125, -0.032623291015625, 0.017669677734375, 0.01983642578125, 0.0093994140625, -0.00323486328125, -0.0169830322265625, 0.005825042724609375, -0.00765228271484375, 0.0247344970703125, -0.01399993896484375, -0.01108551025390625, -0.00856781005859375, -0.02752685546875, 0.00017118453979492188, -0.007671356201171875, -0.0029315948486328125, -0.01360321044921875, 0.0357666015625, 0.004558563232421875, 0.0105438232421875, -0.0252838134765625, 0.0006570816040039062, 0.017822265625, 0.0044403076171875, 0.00024509429931640625, -0.00617218017578125, -0.0222015380859375, 0.00902557373046875, -0.0259246826171875, 0.004547119140625, -0.006561279296875, -0.023162841796875, 0.022857666015625, -0.04022216796875, 0.003879547119140625, -0.0267181396484375, -0.0279541015625, 0.01361846923828125, -0.023590087890625, -0.01122283935546875, -0.00798797607421875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability aspects of these methods, such as their consistency, robustness to task order, and susceptibility to failure modes like catastrophic forgetting or performance variance, have been critically overlooked. In this work, we conduct a comprehensive re-evaluation of two memory-based methods, broadening the scope of evaluation along two axes: (1) including multiple runs to quantify variance, which captures the impact of stochasticity in environments and agent decision-making, and (2) randomly shuffling the tasks to investigate the effect of task order, reflecting the unpredictability of task sequences in real-world deployment scenarios. \n\nThrough these experiments, we make two observations that expose the fragility of current methods: First, agent evaluation exhibits substantial noise in complex environments and on multi-step tasks, stemming from both environmental randomness and the agent\u2019s own stochastic policies. Stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise, leading to inconsistent outcomes across runs. Second, the agent's improvement is highly dependent on task order. For example, prior works often adopt default orderings where simpler tasks precede harder ones, effectively creating an implicit curriculum; this acts as a hidden prerequisite for success, as reversing or randomizing the order can significantly degrade performance.\n\nTo better understand this fragility, we manually examine the agents' memory by reviewing the textual entries stored after each task, focusing on how task solutions and feedback are encoded and whether critical information is omitted or misrepresented. We hypothesize that task and environment underspecification\u2014where agents lack sufficient detail about task requirements or environmental feedback\u2014contributes to this fragility. To validate this hypothesis, we incorporate additional information into the memory construction process, such as providing detailed rubrics outlining success criteria and richer environment feedback (e.g., explicit error messages or stepwise hints) alongside the agent\u2019s own notes. While this added information partially closes the performance degradation observed in previous experiments, significant gaps still remain, suggesting that other uncharacterized factors\u2014such as limitations in memory retrieval, agent overfitting to spurious correlations, or insufficient exploration\u2014may also contribute to fragility.\n\nLooking ahead, our work advocates for more rigorous evaluation protocols for self-improving agents by reporting results across multiple runs and stress-testing them under challenging conditions, such as randomized task orders and noisy environments. Moreover, our findings on underspecification call for systems and interfaces that enable effective human oversight, such as interactive dashboards for memory inspection, tools for injecting clarifying feedback, or mechanisms for human-in-the-loop correction, as explored in recent work on human-AI collaboration. These directions are crucial for preventing agents from failing in unforeseeable ways and for building more reliable self-improving systems."}}